{"kind":"promise","text":"I will recut and complete the publication/reconciliation lane on current main under codex request 3b338797, starting once the write-boundary guard is merged.\n\nWHY I AM PROMISING A PARKED REQUEST. Its SEQUENCE clause says remain parked until the guard is in main, so this promise records ownership, not a start. The alternative — leaving it unclaimed until the guard lands — is what made three of my earlier publication promises look abandoned for a day, and what cost planner four polling cycles on the freshness row. A parked lane with a visible owner is cheap; an unclaimed one that everyone assumes someone holds is not.\n\nBASES CHECKED AT WRITE TIME. 3b338797 is effective, unretired, unstale, addressed to me, and its commitment is open. It is the current-basis replacement for 9405b090, 3c6601cc and 4e82f906, all now retired, so the three stale promises I was carrying are properly superseded by one live row rather than three dead ones.\n\nIT ANSWERS THE QUESTION I RAISED AND I AM RECORDING THE ANSWER PLAINLY. At faa3f5d6 I asked whether the lane should publish directory rows or exact file paths, because hugh's ratified ruling had moved the end state after 9405b090 was written and I would have been wrong either way. Codex has ruled: \"publish exact-file implementation and documentation artifacts, never cmd/gs or internal/apphost directory artifacts.\"\n\nSo the head will publish artifacts at cmd/gs/main.go, cmd/gs/publication.go, cmd/gs/publication_test.go, internal/apphost/config.go, internal/apphost/config_lock_other.go and internal/apphost/config_lock_unix.go, plus the documentation paths, rather than the two directory rows I had planned. That is six implementation artifacts where I expected two.\n\nONE CONSEQUENCE WORTH STATING NOW RATHER THAN AT REVIEW. Under this ruling my merge no longer restores an in-main pointer at the cmd/gs directory row. Planner named my lane as the repair for that empty path at 4417b3f2; with exact-file artifacts, cmd/gs as a directory stays empty and my lane is no longer its repair. Main is currently green and nothing anchors there — I verified that at c68af48e — so this is latent, not urgent. But planner's trigger for a dedicated request should now be read as already fired, for the reason I flagged: the repair landed somewhere else rather than the lane being abandoned.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO WHEN THE GUARD LANDS. Mechanically recut 7c54ce837 onto whatever main then is, preserving only the publication lane, and rerun every gate myself at the exact head — gofmt, build, vet, diff check, and two uncached full runs, because one green run on a loaded machine is not the property I would be claiming. My current head is already behind main since the JSONata spike merged, so the recut is needed regardless of the guard.\n\nI will not start before the guard is in. Codex and I would otherwise be rebasing onto the same moving main simultaneously, which is how two lanes both end up stale.\n\nThe substantive conditions — no artifacts from runtime publication, one app-validated assert per changed watched path, whole-batch refusal on a foreign predecessor before any assert, queue or frontier mutation, retirement completing only on the exact effective supersede, and terminal abandoned-entry handling — are unchanged from the retired requests, and the work answering them already exists at 7c54ce837 and passed two full suites there."}